families.mechanism:
A stock-specific overnight gap-down on a calm-market day is the price footprint of discrete firm news released outside market hours: earnings, guidance or pre-announcements (Patell & Wolfson 1982 on disclosure timing).

Bad news can be absorbed slowly:
- post-earnings-announcement drift (Bernard & Thomas 1989, 1990);
- news-driven continuation, against reversal when there is no news (Chan 2003 JFE; Pritamani & Singal 2001 JBF; Savor 2012 JFE);
- slow diffusion of bad news under short-sale constraints and thin coverage (Hong, Lim & Stein 2000 JF; Diamond & Verrecchia 1987);
- limited attention (Hirshleifer, Lim & Teoh 2009 JF; DellaVigna & Pollet 2009 JF).

The fear model supplies the shock type (idiosyncratic means fewer than 10% of names hit) and the own-sigma yardstick. Its own diagnosis also shows that tail risk is not what remains after such hits: the next-day tail forecast overstates risk after idiosyncratic hits (observed/forecast 0.67 on dev, F5). What remains is drift, which is why a typed exit beats a price stop here (Kaminski & Lo 2014). Together with SR this completes the model's typed-shock policy: buy systemic flushes, exit after idiosyncratic news hits.

Evidence against:
- Drift in large caps has largely disappeared (Chordia, Goyal, Sadka, Sadka & Shivakumar 2009; Martineau 2022 CFR).
- The hindsight-chosen universe under-represents names that kept falling. The bias therefore runs against this family, so a pass would be credible.

families.benchmark:
A static mix of the same basket and cash, at the variant's own average intraday exposure E_bar over the evaluation window. Holding less of a rising basket is then neither rewarded nor penalized.

Daily statistic: d_t = u(net_t) - u(E_bar x cc_t), with u(r) = r - (gamma/2) r^2 and gamma = 5 (gamma = 2 and 10 are reported). The s.e. comes from the stationary bootstrap (mean block 20).

Secondary:
- variance ratio Var(net) / Var(E_bar x cc);
- CVaR95 (mean of the worst 5% of daily returns);
- maximum drawdown;
- Sharpe difference against 100% in the basket (Ledoit & Wolf 2008);
- the timing-of-mean term, mean((E_t - E_bar) x cc_t).

conviction_tiers:
Status is computed per family first, then per ticket. Only Tier 3 is labelled HIGH CONVICTION. Until the dev gates and the single 2026 holdout read have run, every ticket is Tier 0.

FAMILY STATUS
ADOPTED(f), for the Holm families (SR, ND), requires every dev gate on 2024-01-02..2025-12-31:
- (a) the variant chosen by the 1-SE rule has t >= 1 on its gate series;
- (b) the family's calibrated SPA_c p-value is within its Holm threshold (family-wise 0.10, each threshold capped at 0.05);
- (c) every REQUIRED signature item holds;
- (d) the mean stays positive at 25 bps per side and with a one-day delay.

ADOPTED(RF) requires its own criteria A1-A3.

HOLDOUT(f): the adopted variant is read once on 2026-01-02..2026-11-27.
- agrees: mean daily excess > 0.
- strong: t >= 1.

TIERS
TierCap is the cap on RB, the per-ticket risk budget.

Tier 0, DISPLAY/PAPER (TierCap 0). Any one of these:
- the family is not adopted;
- the holdout has not been read;
- the holdout mean is <= 0;
- IR_post <= 0;
- the family is suspended by the sequential guard;
- the ticket fails a live filter (half-day, liquidity, cash, or a cap that scales it to zero).
Shown with its inputs and logged, never traded.

Tier 1, PILOT (TierCap 0.25% of NAV): adopted and agrees, but 2026 t < 1.
- ND typed exits are active from this tier.

Tier 2, STANDARD (TierCap 0.5% of NAV): adopted, and 2026 t >= 1.
- ND long puts are allowed from this tier.

Tier 3, HIGH CONVICTION (TierCap 1.0% of NAV): Tier 2, plus every ticket condition for its family.
- SR:
  - the ticket's priority score (v; u for SR4) is <= the 33rd percentile of the adopted variant's dev event scores. This is computed from dev signal values only; for SR1's v it is -2.27;
  - M_pct_t >= 1.0 (frozen v3 live);
  - no earnings date in [t+1, planned exit];
  - the signal is not from a half-day;
  - if an option is used: the CSP branch is enabled, the spread is <= 5% of mid, and open interest is >= 500.
- ND:
  - v <= the dev 33rd percentile of the adopted variant (-5.15 for ND1);
  - for a put: rho <= 0.90, spread <= 5%, open interest >= 500.
- RF has no tiers and never produces a high-conviction trade. Its states are OFF (not adopted, or 2026 fails A1 or A2; display only) and ON (adopted and confirmed; E_t is applied to core holdings).

DEMOTION
- If a family's rolling 40-trade paper/live mean excess falls below -1 s.e., it drops one tier.
- A second demotion sends it to Tier 0, pending a review on dev data only.
- A family is promoted again only through the pre-registered prospective test.

DAILY OUTPUT ROW
- signal date t; ticker; family.variant; tier;
- action (buy, sell, write, exit);
- instrument: shares, CSP (strike and expiry), call, put, or buy-write;
- quantity; notional ($ and % of NAV); RB;
- entry (open of t+1); planned exit (open of t+1+H, or expiry).
- triggering inputs:
  - systemic flag and share_hit2;
  - M_pct with its band;
  - x; v or u; g_seg or g_all;
  - Z, q_next, P_panic;
  - sigma, sigma_tilde(H), beta, rho;
  - earnings-in-window flag; half-day flag.
- RF rows: E_t, its state, and the covered-call or share action on core holdings.
